---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';

const path = '/use-cases/abandoned-cart';
const title = 'Build a Shopify abandoned-cart recovery workflow';
const desc = 'Define an eligible cart, one message owner and a reliable purchase exit before choosing a schedule.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
<a class="link" href="/use-cases">Explore related guides</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Build a Shopify abandoned-cart recovery workflow</h1>
<p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Define an eligible cart, one message owner and a reliable purchase exit before choosing a schedule.</p>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Define the event boundary</h2>
<p>An item added to a cart is not the same event as a checkout started. <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/migrate-abandoned-checkout">Shopify’s checkout automation documentation</a> describes a shopper who added a product and started checkout before leaving. For an earlier cart reminder, require the proposed app to show its actual event, identity source and eligible audience instead of assuming checkout automation covers anonymous carts.</p>
<h2>Write an enrollment record</h2>
<p>Record the cart or checkout identifier, event timestamp, reachable identity, channel eligibility, selected variants and order lookup. Decide what happens when a second cart arrives: update the existing record, replace it or retain it with a documented reason. Missing identity or channel eligibility should produce an exclusion record, not a guessed address or a new permission assumption.</p>
<h2>Give one system ownership</h2>
<p>List native Shopify recovery and every app that can send a cart reminder. Assign an owner for each event and channel, including migration queues. At checkout transition, stop an earlier cart workflow or explicitly coordinate its next action. Before a send, check current purchase, availability and offer state. Decide how support can pause the queue and who can resume it.</p>
<h2>Use timing to answer a question</h2>
<p>Begin with the store’s observed return pattern and the blocker the message can resolve. A shipping explanation and a size guide serve different questions. Test a delay or message change against the existing path; do not change timing, discount and channel together and then attribute the difference to one factor. Never treat an arbitrary basket average as an incentive cap.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance sequence</h2>
<p>Create an identified cart without checkout, then start checkout, change a variant, and purchase in another session while a reminder waits. Inspect event identity, transition, returned basket and all queued messages. Repeat with an unavailable item and missing channel permission. Save timestamps and exclusion reasons; no execution or passing results are claimed.</p>
<h2>Measure the eligible population</h2>
<p>Separate enrolled, excluded, sent and ordered records. Report attribution rules and a consistent observation window. If using a randomized holdout, choose its size from traffic and the effect you need to detect rather than a universal percentage. Compare contribution after discounts and channel costs, and monitor opt-outs and returns.</p>
<h2>Choose the next guide</h2>
<p>Use <a class="link" href="/guides">the guide directory</a> to investigate a specific blocker or <a class="link" href="/for">store types</a> for catalog constraints. A candidate such as Sequenzy must demonstrate these event and queue requirements; a brand mention does not establish integration coverage.</p>
</article>
</section>
</BaseLayout>
